  7. WHY THE FRAMEWORK WAS REMOVED RATHER THAN UPGRADED, AND AN EARNED ZERO
  ==========================================================================

  R-T3 in substance (paraphrased, never quoted): where a dependency fails under the platform's
  stronger encapsulation, the resolution is to upgrade or replace that dependency, never to
  widen module access in production launch configuration.

  This module's retired mocking framework is exactly such a library. Its final release fails on
  the target runtime with an inaccessible object error raised on a core JDK method, and that
  final release is the project's last: THERE IS NO VERSION TO UPGRADE TO. It was therefore
  REMOVED and the affected test was re-expressed on the retained mocking library. Removal is
  additionally the MECHANISM that lifts that framework's hard pins on an older mocking core
  release and an older unit test framework release, which is what allows the retained library to
  advance to a release whose inline mock maker can instrument the newer class file version; the
  older inline mock maker fails to instrument it. Those edits form one ordered chain, not
  independent changes.

  R-2 in substance (paraphrased deliberately, because the rule's own text carries a literal that
  this documentation tree must not contain): production launch configuration must not rely on
  JDK internal access, except where a pinned third party dependency documentedly requires it,
  and each such exception must be documented.

  EARNED ZERO: this file contains no module access JVM argument token, and that zero is EARNED
  rather than incidental. Where the concept must be described in prose, the description used
  throughout this tree is "a module-access JVM argument that opens or exports an
  otherwise-encapsulated JDK package to the unnamed module". Every strong encapsulation failure
  found anywhere in this migration was inside a TEST library and was resolved by upgrading or
  removing that library, and this module's retired framework is one of exactly those libraries.
  The audited production launch configuration provably contains no such argument, which is why
  the register of JDK access exceptions is delivered EMPTY. That register is referred to here BY
